WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.972 --> 00:00:04.680
PROFESSOR: There are
four ways that chefs

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.470
use to control the
viscosity- their reductions,

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:12.480
their starch-based thickener,
there's making a emulsion by adding

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.300
fat, or using modernist thickeners,
where small amounts of material,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.330
small amounts of a polymer,
are added to increase

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.940
the viscosity by large amounts.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.740
In all cases, what we're doing
is adding something to the fluid.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.610
In the case of reduction, we're
reducing the amount of liquid.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.120
And what remains are particles,
are nonvolatile solids.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.890
This increases the concentration,
or the concentration of the solids,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.330
in the liquid.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.960
In the case of starch, we're
adding starch granules.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.990
In the case of fat, or making an
emulsion, we're adding drops of fat.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.420
And the drops of fat
behave just like particles.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.690
In the case of the modernist
thickeners, we're adding polymers.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.600
And they also have the effect of
adding something to the fluid.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.500
And this is what increases
the viscosity of the fluid.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.760
The simplest way to understand this,
is to think of particles in the fluid.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.850
When the fluid flows,
the particles also flow.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:17.490
And they have to flow over one another,
in order for the whole fluid to flow.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.700
The way they behave depends
on the concentration

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.130
of the particles in the fluid.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.640
It depends on the volume of
the particles, or the volume

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.340
that the particles occupy in the fluid.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.120
This is called volume fraction, or
the volume fraction of particles,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.710
in the fluid.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.780
We can calculate that easily.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.570
We calculate the volume
of each of the particles,

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.490
and we take the total number of
particles times their individual volume

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.760
and divide that by the
total volume of the fluid.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.980
When there are very few
particles in the fluid,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.290
or their volume fraction is very low,
then they have almost no effect at all.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.790
When the fluid flows, the
particles just flow with the fluid.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:02.950
However, when the volume fraction
of the particles increases,

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.650
then they become crowded.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.940
It's more difficult for the
particles to flow over one another.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.460
And they collide with each other,
they move on top of each other.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:17.160
They take much more volume,
and the flow is restricted.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.750
In this case, the particles really
impact the flow of the fluid

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.150
and increase the viscosity of the fluid.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.740
However, for that to happen,
the volume fraction of particles

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.360
has to be quite high.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.220
It has to be sufficiently high that
they can't easily flow over one another.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.480
This doesn't happen unless the
volume fraction of the particles

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.460
gets to be of the order of 50%,
or half the volume of the fluid

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.660
is occupied by the particles.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.830
When the volume fraction is
so high, then the particles

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.150
can't easily flow over one another.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.980
Now we can understand in a very
simple way why starch is so

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.010
effective at increasing the viscosity.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.040
First, the starch granules expand.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.080
They absorb water, and they get larger.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.040
As we show in this little
cartoon, the volume gets larger.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.210
And so, the volume fraction,
the effective volume fraction

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.940
of the starch granules, increases.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.760
And while you may
start out with only 15%

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.060
volume fraction, when
they've expanded, then they

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.240
can occupy a much
larger volume fraction.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.860
The second way that starch can
increase the viscosity of the fluid

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.740
is that the granules stick together.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.540
In that case, they form these more
tenuous structures, as we show here.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.150
The volume of these
tenuous structures is

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.930
the volume of the structures
themselves plus all the water that they

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.750
enclose, as shown by this circle.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.290
And that's how you can greatly
increase the viscosity of a fluid,

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:53.550
even if you don't have,
essentially, 50% of the volume.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.730
You have 50% of the volume
fraction of particles,

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.900
because the particles occupy a
much larger effective volume.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:07.080
The last way to increase the viscosity,
is to add these modernist thickeners.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.610
What they are, are large
molecules or polymers.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:15.660
And in that case, only a very,
very small amount of material

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.640
can lead to a large
increase in the viscosity.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.480
The way these work is actually
very similar to way the particles

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.050
work, except that polymers
have a unique feature

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.820
that they can occupy a huge
volume with very little mass.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:37.850
To understand that, let's understand
the way a polymer actually behaves.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.970
A polymer is a very long,
but very flexible, molecule,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.060
and is made up of many, many monomers.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.450
And for a polymer,
because it's so flexible,

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:53.610
the bond between each of the
monomers can bend in any direction.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.200
It can bend either way.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.000
It can bend one way or the other way.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.090
And it does that, with
equal probability.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:03.820
This has very much the appearance
of a random [? walk. ?]

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.600
Here's an image of a
random walk of an ion.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.130
It can go in any direction.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:13.300
It goes randomly in all
different directions.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:18.430
This random walk is exactly what
describes the behavior of a polymer.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:24.100
And, in fact, we can lay the polymer
shape directly onto this random walk.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:29.290
So the same way we describe the random
walk that describes diffusion of ions

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.810
through a fluid, that's
the way we describe

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:35.560
the shape of a large polymer molecule.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.800
What this means is that the
polymer molecule actually

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.630
takes up a huge amount of volume,
for a very small amount of mass.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:48.610
Yet because the polymer spreads and
fills the water with this polymer,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.970
it can increase its viscosity
by a very large amount.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:56.770
Because the polymers occupy so
little actual volume of the fluid,

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:58.420
they can entangle.

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:03.310
They can come together and
intertwine among each other.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.680
That means the effective
volume of the polymers

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.260
is actually much greater than one.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:11.650
And the viscosity is even larger.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:14.950
Remember that the polymers have
to get out of the way of one

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.130
another, in order for the fluid to flow.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:24.190
Now they have to disentangle for
them to get out of each other's way.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:29.000
That's called reptation, and
that's a very, very slow process.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.660
You can think of it like
a bowl of spaghetti.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:36.400
If you want to take one spaghetti out,
you have to pull along its length.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.350
And that takes a long time.

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:42.280
And that's why these thickeners,
these polymer thickeners,

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.450
are so effective at increasing
the viscosity of a fluid.

